			<div class="content" id="post-content-4843486">
									

<p>Giving crit cuz I was sniped on the idea forums</p>
<p>howdy, I'm yellow, and ima be dropping a critique today.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">lbl</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The body was found at 4:13pm on a Sunday.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is definitely a trope, and I love that trope, but it does make readers roll their eyes sometimes. Be wary.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If you could even call it that.</p>
<p>A body, I mean.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Making these all into separate lines feels like it is trying to add emphasis where it <em>really isn't needed.</em> I understand the intro with the whole &quot;hook in&quot; part, but making these two lines doesn't feel terribly necessary.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When the first unwitting civilian stumbled upon it, it was still a steaming pile of viscera rendered inside-out by some unknown force, fragmented polygons of ivory bone jutting through the pulped remains of flesh and muscle, sinew and organs.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is all one sentence. This could use breaking up <em>at least</em> into two sentences.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Remarkably, the extremities were intact: hands and wrists, shins and feet, the neck and head.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a long list giving emphasis, yes, but the way it is formatted <em>really</em> irks with me. Commas are present at some points yet aren't?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>By the time I arrived, the blood had coagulated like carmine veins in the cracks of the flagstones. A dank and metallic smell lingered about the scene my cohorts had established with their cheery yellow markers.<br />
We were quick to establish ground here ahead of civilian police. This was far beyond their jurisdiction.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm liking this part, but this all could be one paragraph.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>My senior officer - Officer Dalton - gave me the details, and we discussed the evidence.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You could honestly state My senior officer Dalton and have the same feel here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Officer Dalton hums and haws a bit. “I suppose.”</p>
</blockquote>
<p>hums and haws feels weird here, but I suppose it works. I will note that using &quot;they&quot; would be a lot less clunky, and make the sentence flow better.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>“None that we could find. What’s your ChemAnalysis say?”</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Consider Chem-Analysis</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The air was damp, so it lingered, and even an unmodified person could easily tell.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What does &quot;unmodified&quot; mean in this context? Consider a different word usage.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>“Nothing. No explosive residue or anything. May I -?” I gesture to the remains.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>When it comes to cut-offs, typically, the paragraph ends there. Also, a cut off doesn't work the best here. Consider the following:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>“Nothing. No explosive residue or anything.&quot; I gesture my body to the remains. &quot;May I?&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>I make my way over, stepping around various items of gore and little yellow placards.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>what does &quot;various items of gore&quot; refer to? The grammar here needs re-wording.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A quick scan with my eyepiece maps the blood-spattered albeit intact face to an online repository of personnel records - and finds a match.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This sentence pacing goes by very fast, and needs a comma.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>“Interesting they left all of the uh, identifying components intact,” I say aloud to nobody in particular. “We got a matching face ID, prolly fingerprints too if we took’em.”</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Very strange use of dialogue here, feels more fake, or a thought, over a real person speaking to themself. When people speak to themselves, they typically tend to be more realistic with themself, like speaking to another, but on a deeper level.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>“Your job is literally full-time Zombie.”</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Grammar is clunky here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The on-call medic hovers about like a nervous fly, until I wave him off.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>happens way to fast, needs better pacing. Break into two sentences is my best advice.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The brain had already begun to decay and so, much like a VCR tape degraded through magnetic interference, the images began to decay with it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Slow down pacing here, this is on the verge of being a run-on sentence.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Despite my own thoughts being in-tact I could feel the ghost of the other mind’s presence alongside my own, unaware of its death and guiding the body through the busy street as it could remember doing.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Problems with this sentence:</p>
<ul>
<li>in-tact should be intact</li>
<li>run on sentence</li>
<li>fast paced sentence, needs more commas</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>As I mused this the world shifted around me, twisting in on itself and re-orientating as the dimly-lit interior of a dated café.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>These should be two separate sentences, one for each concept in the sentence.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The body looks up, and sat in front of us is an attractive albeit ordinary-looking woman</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You use the term &quot;albeit&quot; a lot, and whenever you use that word, you use a double comma. Example:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>He was strong, albeit lanky and undertoned.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>“I wanted to discuss a deal,” She says, the cadence of her voice song-like and airy. “We are aware of your expertise, Mr. Rutkowski, and we believe that there may be mutual benefits to be found.”</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't like this part. It feels fake and forced, a rather underwhelming interpretation of professionalism.</p>
<hr />
<p>Quickly noting this because I don't want to be noting this throughout the entirety of the lbl. The dialogue pacing feels incredibly <em>forced</em>, and I never felt immersed in the characters. It feels as though I am simply learning the information on its own, and not hearing two distinct and real characters conversing with each other. I recommend going back and redoing the dialogue, and consider reading it aloud, as that helps catch and distinctify forced dialogue. Dialogue is the focal point of this article to me, but as of now, it is somewhat underwhelming, and could use reworking.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>“We are just…mmm, well informed.”</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;mmm&quot; is a strange onamonapia</p>
<blockquote>
<p>“ - forget it. I can’t - I can’t do this not knowing what’s gonna happen to me. You could be lying. I won’t.”</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The grammar here is poor, but that is acceptable seeing the current predicament. However, I will not that this is paced <em>incredibly</em> strangely for a situation like this. I strongly urge you to make them speak much more slower then normal, as if they are trying to put together thoughts.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The gentle clack-clack-clack of footsteps was almost palpable, down the alley.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Remove comma, yet another onamonapia, which feels off for an article like this.</p>
<p>No longer doing line by line beyond this point, I felt disinterest beyond here, and will read to finish and give overall concept review.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<hr />
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">concept/overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<h5><span>Concept</span></h5>
<blockquote>
<p>My senior officer - Officer Dalton - gave me the details, and we discussed the evidence.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This part irks with me a lot. An entire bit of dialogue has been replaced with a single line, when dialogue could be used here to help build up the world surrounding this tale. I recommend changing this to dialogue, and giving more depth to the conversation beyond the following three lines. The discussion of evidence could help delve deeper into the story itself.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It must’ve rained at some point during his trip.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't wish to break this to you, but rain is the one thing that breaks immersion the most. When it rains onto people, they typically break any &quot;immersion&quot; they have in what they are doing, and tend to note the rain. It's a natural biological response from the body. Even when in crisis, people are able to distinguish different liquids splashing onto their bodies. Look it up, it's a real thing. In any case, the part about being wet is somewhat unnecessary, so the best way to explain this would to be omitting the concept as a whole.</p>
<hr />
<hr />
<h5><span>Overall</span></h5>
<p>I would like to begin by noting that I specifically <em>did not</em> read through the idea pitch in the forums (partially because I did not have the time to). I would have gone back and read it, but I believe that your article should be able to convey the same experience to me without having to read anything beyond it. For this reason, I am giving critique based without the knowledge of events.</p>
<p>Right away, I want to note on something that irked me a lot when reading; The ending.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The last thing I remember is the medic’s heavy hand on my shoulder, and his voice, barely audible.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This felt so&#8230; underwhelming? I felt like the ending would have dragged on at least a <em>bit</em> more, but I felt dissatisfied when finishing this. The entirety of the article was fairly okay, but the ending disappointed me when reading through. I wished for it to be a bit more &quot;huzzah&quot;, more concluding then &quot;the last thing I remembered.&quot; I suggest adding a line similar to the common trope of &quot;then everything went black.&quot; etc. It works very well in these situations, adding a sort of &quot;cliffhanger&quot; which provides a nice concluding ending.</p>
<p>Concept-wise, there were points where I was confused, but I can do very little to help with the concept. Line by line is my strength. However, I will note that it could be reworded a lot to help readers understand what you are saying better. An example of this would be your extreme use of metaphor. It was nice, but it could be cut down upon quite a hefty amount.</p>
<p>However, as an article, the core concept was fairly decent. Very nice concept with a easy to follow working.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<hr />
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">format&nbsp;tingz</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>If you could even call it that.<br />
A body, I mean.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Break up by one more line. This happens a lot, but for both reader convenience and consistency, break all paragraphs up by two lines:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>like</p>
<p>this</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>INITIALISING NECRO-NEURAL INTERROGATION</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>INTENTIONS FOUND TO BE IN VIOLATION OF THE 2027 NEUROTRANSMISSION ACT WILL BE MET WITH DISCIPLINARY ACTIONS AS DESCRIBED THEREIN</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>… INITIALISATION COMPLETE</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't particularly like how these are all separate, especially since nothing happens in between these events. I suggest putting things in between over making it into one box, as one box will make this clunky, and the wording adds character.</p>
<hr />
<p>Hmm. A majority of the paragraph breaks in this article feel unjustified, adding emphasis to specific points and dividing up reader concepts. I suggest making this more paragraph-based, as having a separate line this many times really messes with the reader POV.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>“We are just…mmm, well informed.”</p>
</blockquote>
<p>do this instead (for &#8230;)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>“We are just… mmm, well informed.”</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>“ - forget it. I can’t - I can’t do this not knowing what’s gonna happen to me. You could be lying. I won’t.”</p>
</blockquote>
<p>change hyphens</p>
<blockquote>
<p>“-forget it. I can’t- I can’t do this not knowing what’s gonna happen to me. You could be lying. I won’t.”</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</div>
</div>

